</p>
<p>No. If each of the 13 reels must end with the tape it started with, then each of these reels must go through an even number of tape transfers. The empty reel, too, must go through an even number of transfers to remain empty. But each of the 13 tapes must be wound or rewound an odd number of times to end up reversed. These facts can&#8217;t be reconciled, so the task can&#8217;t be done.</p>
<p>From Dick Hess&#8217; excellent <a href="https://archive.org/details/allstarmathletep0000hess/page/12/mode/2up"><em>All-Star Mathlete Puzzles</em></a>, 2009.</p>
